## Theory 5: Prometheus scrape storm — REJECTED as primary cause

### Evidence FOR:
- Prometheus scrapes all nodes + all pods continuously
- kube-state-metrics scrape is expensive (LIST operations against etcd)
- Large scrape responses could cause read bursts

### Evidence AGAINST:
- Prometheus pod log shows last restart was 2026-04-23T12:17:23Z
  (Apr 23 15:17 local) — ran continuously for 14 hours before first spike
- No "Start listening" or init events immediately before either spike
- Steady-state scraping is absorbed fine (baseline PSI 1-3%)
- Spikes are intermittent, scraping is constant

### Retained possibility:
Prometheus could be an AMPLIFIER during cascades. When apiserver is
briefly slow, Prometheus retries pile up, and retries all fire when
apiserver recovers. This would exacerbate but not cause spikes.

### Mitigation if needed:
- Increase scrape intervals for kube-state-metrics (15s → 30s or 60s)
- Add relabel configs to drop expensive metrics
- Use recording rules for heavy queries

## Theory 6: Host overcommit / VM memory undersizing — CONFIRMED CONTRIBUTOR

### Evidence FOR:
- Host has 22-23 GB RAM, VMs allocated 23,808 MB (104% committed at start)
- k8s-master1 had only 2.5 GB RAM (2.2 GB actual after overhead)
- 0 swap inside VM
- Committed_AS on master1 was 4.7 GB (213% commit within VM)
- kube-apiserver repeatedly OOMKilled (exit 137) — confirmed via
  `/registry/events` showing exitCode 137 and restart count 86
- MemAvailable on master1 was 621 MB during investigation
- Host `top` showed 363 MB free later in session
- kswapd0 at 9.3% CPU (kernel swap daemon active)
- kcompactd0 at 2.3% CPU (memory compaction under pressure)
- Memory pressure graph on Proxmox appeared for first time at 03:09

### Evidence AGAINST as SOLE cause:
- Spikes happened for 2 weeks without obvious correlation to memory
  usage curve
- Changing memory from 2.5 → 3 GB did not prevent 03:09 spike
- Host-side overcommit existed for same 2 weeks

### Revised understanding:
Memory pressure is definitely a contributor, definitely increases
severity when it occurs, but is not by itself the trigger for every
spike. Necessary but not sufficient.

### Fix attempted tonight:
- Masters: 2.5 GB → 3 GB (balloon disabled, full allocation)
- Workers: 3.25 GB → 3 GB
- Net effect: host more overcommitted by ~750 MB
- This change is INSUFFICIENT — should have been 4 GB masters
- Worker reduction may have worsened worker-side pressure

## Theory 7: NVMe contention among multiple etcd instances — CONFIRMED MECHANISM

### Evidence FOR:
- 3 etcd instances on same NVMe doing small random reads/writes
- During spikes, all 3 masters' disk devices (dm-23, dm-24, dm-25) show
  simultaneous activity
- Peak sample: NVMe r_await 151ms, queue depth 143, util 94%
- etcd bbolt DB reads are mmap'd random reads
- Without adequate page cache (small VM RAM), every read goes to disk
- Disk IS fine but becomes saturated under this specific workload

### Evidence for the feedback loop:
```
Small VM RAM → etcd DB doesn't fit in page cache
             → every read hits NVMe
             → NVMe load multiplied 10-100x vs cached etcd
             → NVMe queue builds → latency climbs
             → etcd apply slows → apiserver DeadlineExceeded
             → controllers fail liveness → restart cascade
             → restarting controllers do full LIST
             → etcd reads surge again → feedback
```

### Mitigation path:
Enough RAM in VMs for etcd DB + working set to live in page cache.
With etcd DB typically 200-500 MB, need at least 2-3 GB of *free*
memory inside VM after all other K8s components. Requires 4-6 GB
master VMs minimum.

## Theory 8: master1 hung for hours before reboot — CONFIRMED EVENT

### Evidence FOR:
- User reported master1 was unreachable "since morning"
- User manually rebooted at 02:51
- 03:09 spike is partly the rejoin storm
- Etcd catch-up with peers after extended downtime requires reading
  WAL delta, can be hundreds of MB
- Six KVM processes showing sequential PIDs (382482-383375) all at
  40-67% CPU confirms multiple VMs started recently

### Root cause of hang — NOT YET CONFIRMED:
Most probable: OOM kill cascade inside VM with no swap. With
kube-apiserver, etcd, kubelet, containerd, multiple Calico
components all competing for 2.5 GB, OOM killer fires, takes out
apiserver (biggest consumer), system tries to recover, fires again.
Eventually enough critical processes are killed or the scheduler
locks up.

